// One row of one glyph, packed into an int.

// nb bytes, most significant byte first, so the leftmost column of the glyph
// ends up at bit nb*8-1 -- which is the bit txt_px_sc shifts against, because
// txt_bit() is that same nb*8. The byte order inside a row is NOT the file's
// little-endian field order: the fields are numbers and the rows are pixels, and
// pixels are always drawn left to right from the top bit. Reading the rows
// little-endian mirrors every wide glyph, which on an 8-wide face is invisible
// and on a 12-wide one is the only visible symptom.
psf_row(int sa, int nb) {
  int v = 0;
  int i = 0;
  while (i < nb) {
    v = v * 256 + asset_at(sa + i);
    i = i + 1;
  }
} return int v;
